<title>GERMANY: German banks - EMU doesn't require strict deficits.</title>
<headline>German banks - EMU doesn't require strict deficits.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1997-06-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Association of German Banks said on Tuesday it backed a more flexibile interpretation of entry criteria for the single European currency.</p>
<p>&quot;It would be fatal if the euro were to collapse if the German (budget) deficit was at 3.2 percent (of gross domestic product)&quot; the association's leader, Manfred Weber, told a news conference.</p>
<p>Weber said the Maastricht Treaty does not call for an exact budget deficit limit of 3.0 percent, adding sustainable financial stability was more critical.</p>
<p>Germany is at risk in 1997 of failing to meet the 3.0 percent of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) deficit goal that German Finance Minister Theo Waigel has said is critical for entry to the single European currency.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
